<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oikonomos">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oikonomos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as oeconomus, œconomus, economos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oikonomos, household manager, was the ancient Greek word for the person in charge of all the affairs of an oikos, the household, the base unit of Greek social and economic life. Usually the husband, he made its economic decisions and acted as kyrios, legal guardian, of his wife, unmarried women and males under 18, arranging marriages and representing them at law; to the household's slaves he was not kyrios but despotes, master. He also directed the labour of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a> and represented the household in the polis. In Byzantine times the word became a title for the manager or treasurer of an organisation, and it is still a title in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches.</p></div>
